{
  "id": "task_008",
  "title": "Matrix Chain with Forbidden Splits",
  "language": "python",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "source": "Custom \u2014 matrix chain multiplication + forbidden pairs",
  "description": "Write a Python function `matrix_chain(dims: list[int], forbidden: list[list[int]]) -> int`.\n\nGiven `n` matrices where matrix `i` has dimensions `dims[i] x dims[i+1]`, find the minimum number of scalar multiplications to compute their product.\n\nHowever, certain split points are **forbidden**: `forbidden[k] = [i, j]` means you may NOT use the split where the last multiplication combines the subchain `[i..k]` with `[k+1..j]` for any `k` in `[i, j-1]`. In other words, the subchain `[i..j]` (0-indexed) cannot be computed as a single direct product of two sub-results \u2014 it must be split differently.\n\nReturn the minimum cost, or -1 if it is impossible to compute the full product under the given constraints.\n\nFunction signature:\ndef matrix_chain(dims: list[int], forbidden: list[list[int]]) -> int:",
  "function_signature": "def matrix_chain(dims: list[int], forbidden: list[list[int]]) -> int:",
  "constraints": [
    "2 <= len(dims) <= 15 (so 1 to 14 matrices)",
    "1 <= dims[i] <= 100",
    "0 <= len(forbidden) <= 20",
    "forbidden[k] = [i, j] with 0 <= i < j <= n-1"
  ],
  "test_cases": [
